import Mathlib

/-!
# A315501: coordination sequence Gal.4.149.3

Source: https://oeis.org/A315501 and Brian Galebach, k-uniform tilings (k <= 6) and their
A-numbers, https://oeis.org/A250120/a250120.html (tiling #241, 4-uniform tiling 149 of 151,
standard notation [(3^6)^3; 3^4.6]).

Conjectures stated here (OEIS A315501, FORMULA section):
  Conjectures from Chai Wah Wu, Jun 16 2026: a(n) = 2*a(n-5) - a(n-10) for n > 13. G.f.: (x + 1)^2*(-x^11 + x^10 - x^9 + 2*x^8 + 4*x^7 + 3*x^6 + 8*x^5 + 5*x^4 + 8*x^3 + 2*x^2 + 4*x + 1)/(x^10 - 2*x^5 + 1).

The tiling. Galebach gives each vertex type as its cyclic sequence of polygons together with,
for every edge, the type of the neighbour and its rotation (a prime marks a reflection):
  Gal.4.149.1: A: 6.3^4; A 60; A 300; B 300; C 0; B 0
  Gal.4.149.2: B: 3^6; C 60; A 60; A 0; C 0; D 0; D 240
  Gal.4.149.3: C: 3^6; B 0; A 0; B 300; D 180; C 180; D 0
  Gal.4.149.4: D: 3^6; D 240; B 0; C 0; C 180; B 120; D 120
All edges have length 1. Rebuilding the tiling from this notation gives a periodic graph.
Its translation lattice has basis t1 = (-5/2, (5√3)/2) and t2 = (-5, 0).
The vertex (x, y, i) is the point x t1 + y t2 + p_i, where p_i and the vertex type are:
  i = 0: p_i = (-5, √3), type A
  i = 1: p_i = (-4, 0), type A
  i = 2: p_i = (-4, √3), type A
  i = 3: p_i = (-7/2, (√3)/2), type A
  i = 4: p_i = (-1/2, (√3)/2), type A
  i = 5: p_i = (0, 0), type A
  i = 6: p_i = (-9/2, (3√3)/2), type B
  i = 7: p_i = (-3, 0), type B
  i = 8: p_i = (-3, √3), type B
  i = 9: p_i = (-2, 2√3), type B
  i = 10: p_i = (-1, 0), type B
  i = 11: p_i = (-1, √3), type B
  i = 12: p_i = (-6, 2√3), type C
  i = 13: p_i = (-11/2, (3√3)/2), type C
  i = 14: p_i = (-7/2, (3√3)/2), type C
  i = 15: p_i = (-3, 2√3), type C
  i = 16: p_i = (-5/2, (√3)/2), type C
  i = 17: p_i = (-3/2, (√3)/2), type C
  i = 18: p_i = (-5, 2√3), type D
  i = 19: p_i = (-4, 2√3), type D
  i = 20: p_i = (-5/2, (3√3)/2), type D
  i = 21: p_i = (-2, 0), type D
  i = 22: p_i = (-2, √3), type D
  i = 23: p_i = (-3/2, (3√3)/2), type D
`adj i` lists the neighbours of (0, 0, i) as triples (dx, dy, j). The edge set is invariant
under the translations, so (x, y, i) is adjacent exactly to the (x + dx, y + dy, j).
From each vertex type this graph reproduces the 50 terms of the coordination sequence in
Galebach table, and from the base vertex (0, 0, 12) (type C) the data of A315501.

The graph table was generated by a script (written with the help of an AI agent, Claude Code)
from Galebach expanded notation, with exact coordinates in Z[ζ12].
-/

namespace OMA.S_oeis_a315501

/-- `(x, y, i)` is the vertex `i` of the fundamental cell, translated by `x t1 + y t2`. -/
abbrev Vertex := ℤ × ℤ × Fin 24

/-- The neighbours of `(0, 0, i)`, as `(dx, dy, j)`. -/
def adj : Fin 24 → List (ℤ × ℤ × Fin 24) := ![
  [(0, 0, 2), (0, 0, 6), (0, 0, 13), (0, 1, 4), (0, 1, 11)],
  [(-1, 0, 12), (-1, 1, 9), (0, 0, 3), (0, 0, 7), (0, 1, 5)],
  [(0, 0, 0), (0, 0, 3), (0, 0, 6), (0, 0, 8), (0, 0, 14)],
  [(0, 0, 1), (0, 0, 2), (0, 0, 7), (0, 0, 8), (0, 0, 16)],
